我希望提供的信号不要太强烈，以免让我感到疲劳或习惯它。当我接收听觉信息时，我希望这些听觉信息是直接针对我的。这些信息是有意义的，意味着我需要采取行动。我们可以利用医院的声景做得更多。研究人员正在寻找方法，为那些失去声音的人赋予声音。

28
00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:45.000
You think of a patient in the ICU when they're non-communicative or in long-term acute care, those patients often get neglected, but they have ways to communicate in a non-traditional sense, for example, through their autonomic nervous system. Patients who can't speak or communicate their feelings are still expressing themselves through their heart rate or their body temperature. So they're communicating but not in a traditional verbal sense. How can we take those physiologic signals, turn them into sound in a way that we can start to ascertain what these patients, what these people are saying.
你可以想想ICU里的患者，当他们无法交流或处于长期急性护理中时，这些患者往往会被忽视，但他们有非传统意义的交流方式，例如通过他们的自主神经系统。那些无法说话或表达自己感受的患者仍然在通过他们的心率或体温来表达自己。所以他们是在交流，但不是通过传统的言语方式。我们如何才能将这些生理信号转化为声音，从而开始弄清楚这些患者、这些人到底在说些什么？

29
00:13:45.000 --> 00:14:11.000
The result is something called biomew music, pioneered by a colleague of Joe's at McGill University in Montreal. Sensors on the patient's body detect vital signs, and that makes the data into sound. Heart rate controls the tempo, temperature determines the notes, and skin sweat drives the melody. The result is actually kind of beautiful. Biomusic is being looked at as a way for non-verbal autistic children to express themselves.
其结果被称为“生物音乐”（biomusic），由乔在蒙特利尔麦吉尔大学的一位同事首创。患者身体上的传感器检测生命体征，并将数据转化为声音。心率控制节奏，体温决定音符，皮肤出汗驱动旋律。其结果其实相当美观。人们正将生物音乐视为非语言自闭症儿童表达自己的一种方式。

30
00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:45.000
Here's a calm state of mind. And here's an anxious one. You don't have to be a medical specialist to hear the difference. Our hope is that we can make it easy to implement throughout the world where you don't have to be at a high-powered academic institution with fancy EEG equipment to make this a reality. Joel Buckerman believes our hospital soundscapes can be improved from an unhealthy and dangerous cacophony to
这里是一个平静的心态。这里是一个焦虑的心态。你不需要成为医学专家就能听出其中的区别。我们的希望是，我们可以使其易于在世界各地实施，你不必身处拥有昂贵脑电图（EEG）设备的高精尖学术机构，也能让这一愿景成为现实。乔尔·贝克曼认为，我们的医院声景可以从不健康且危险的噪声改善为

